teather (0.1.0-13) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fixed a self-heal wedge: after a failed connect or auto-connect the daemon
    latched the raw error category it hit (for example "dns-residue" from
    preflight) rather than "recovery-pending". The poll-loop reconcile only
    re-ran while the category was "recovery-pending", so it went dormant and the
    daemon stayed stuck in "error" indefinitely — even once the underlying
    residue had cleared — until a manual service restart. Reconcile now self-
    heals on any error state.
  * recover() can now clear an orphaned Teather DNS sentinel. When resolv.conf
    still lists 198.19.0.1 but no teather0 connection remains (a stale file
    NetworkManager had not regenerated after an unclean shutdown), recover()
    asks NetworkManager to rewrite resolv.conf instead of failing until NM
    happens to refresh it on its own.
  * The GTK window now sets its icon name and app id ("teather") so the Wayland
    shell / X11 window switcher shows the Teather icon instead of the generic
    fallback; teather.desktop gained a matching StartupWMClass.

 -- Teather Project <noreply@example.invalid>  Wed, 03 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

teather (0.1.0-12) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The Android relay now requires SOCKS5 username/password authentication
    (RFC 1929) with a per-run secret (D-028). The relay listens on the phone's
    loopback, which every installed app can reach regardless of its permissions;
    without auth any of them could use the relay as an open proxy on the phone's
    cellular link (including apps with no INTERNET permission). The phone
    generates a fresh 128-bit secret each time the relay starts and publishes it
    only in the DUMP-protected status, so only adb/root can learn it. teatherd
    reads it and hands it to tun2proxy. Status schema is now 2; a schema-1 relay
    and this client will not pair. Requires the Teather app at versionCode 5 /
    0.1.0-p1.3.
  * Hardened the udpgw frame parser: a data frame with a truncated address block
    now raises a clean protocol error instead of an unchecked exception.
  * Fixed a race in the relay's shutdown that could throw (and leave sockets
    open) when the last session closed concurrently with Socks5Server.close().
  * The package now bundles the matching Android APK (D-029). 'teather device
    install' installs it on a connected phone, or upgrades it when the phone's
    app is older than the one this package carries — which keeps the two halves
    on the same status schema. The daemon never installs on its own; the CLI
    confirms first. New D-Bus methods AndroidAppState and InstallAndroid.
  * The Android app has a "Get the desktop client" button that opens the
    project's releases page.
  * Release signing is now wired (D-030): app/build.gradle.kts uses a gitignored
    keystore.properties or TEATHER_KEYSTORE* env vars, falling back to the debug
    key with a warning. build-deb.sh prefers a release-signed APK.

 -- Teather Project <noreply@example.invalid>  Mon, 01 Sep 2026 12:00:00 +0000

teather (0.1.0-11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Self-healing after an abnormal disconnect (D-026). If the phone is
    unplugged, the USB/ADB bridge drops, tun2proxy exits, NetworkManager drops
    teather0, or the phone-side relay stops, teatherd now notices, releases its
    own resources, and returns to a clean state that auto-connect brings
    straight back when the phone reappears — no manual 'teather recover'. The
    phone-relay check is a slow (~2 min) backstop, skipped entirely while a GUI
    is polling status, so it adds no steady load. Auto-connect stops retrying
    after 3 consecutive failures and rests until a replug, a manual Connect, or
    a 2-minute pause elapses, instead of looping. The startup session check
    logs its result and surfaces an unclearable state. Phone-side cleanup that cannot be confirmed (a forward
    that will not remove, a relay that will not confirm stopped) is logged and
    surfaced as a hint but never wedges the next connect; only an unverifiable
    teather0 (host state) still asks for a restart. New get_status fields
    'recovery_hint' and 'last_drop'.
  * Persistent debug log at ~/.local/state/teather/teatherd.log (rotating,
    0600, StateDirectory=teather). Every adb control command, D-Bus method,
    connect/disconnect/reconcile/health step and NetworkManager action is
    recorded; browsing destinations and resolver contents are not. TEATHER_DEBUG=1
    raises it to DEBUG.
  * Desktop notifications on connect, drop, self-heal, and needs-attention, so
    state changes are visible with the window closed and no tray. Sent at
    critical urgency with replace-in-place, so they raise a banner (GNOME does
    not banner normal-urgency notifications) and never stack up.
  * GTK app is now single-instance: launching teather-gtk again re-presents the
    existing window. Closing the window never disconnects; it hides to the tray
    when one is available.
  * After a standalone activation (Teather the only link) teatherd nudges
    NetworkManager's connectivity check so GNOME shows a network icon
    (punch-list item 5).

 -- Teather Project <noreply@example.invalid>  Sun, 31 Aug 2026 22:00:00 +0000

teather (0.1.0-10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Recover cleanly after a teatherd restart during a live connection
    (punch-list item 4). ADB cleanup is now idempotent: removing a forward that
    is already gone and stopping a relay that is already stopped both count as
    success. Startup recover() clears the ownership journal once the
    NetworkManager/tunnel teardown is verified, even when the best-effort ADB
    step cannot be, so 'teather connect' is no longer wedged with
    recovery-pending until the journal file is deleted by hand.

